<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">French Committee of National Liberation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1943">1943</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The French Committee of National Liberation was formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> in June 1943, uniting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>'s Free French with the forces of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-giraud">Henri Giraud</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. It acted as the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> outside German control, directing French forces and the Resistance, and de Gaulle soon became its sole leader. In June 1944 it became the Provisional Government of the French Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-committee-of-national-liberation.jpg" alt="French Committee of National Liberation" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_Gaulle-Giraud_shot0092.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1940, Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-petain">Philippe Pétain</a> took full power, signed an armistice with Nazi Germany and set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> regime, which governed unoccupied southern and central France and most of the overseas territories. Free France, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, became a government in exile, but neither the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> nor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> officially recognised it.</p><p>In November 1942 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> invaded French North Africa. Vichy forces resisted until Admiral François Darlan ordered them to join the Allies, who recognised him as ruler of the region; Darlan named General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-giraud">Henri Giraud</a> commander-in-chief of the army. Free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had not been told of the invasion or of this Darlan deal. After Darlan was assassinated in December, Giraud ruled with the Vichy officials in place and resisted de Gaulle's call for a purge of collaborators, though he grew more critical of Vichy, notably in a speech written by his adviser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-monnet">Jean Monnet</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The committee was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> on 3 June 1943 with Giraud and de Gaulle as co-presidents. Its charter promised to re-establish all French liberties, the laws of the Republic and the republican regime, and it declared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> regime illegitimate for its collaboration with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, then counted part of metropolitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the liberated parts of the colonial empire.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> at first treated it as a wartime body with limited functions rather than a future government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It expanded its membership, built its own administration and set up the Provisional Consultative Assembly. De Gaulle outmanoeuvred Giraud politically, and on 9 November 1943 the committee came under his sole chairmanship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the Allied landings in Normandy the committee moved its headquarters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and in August 1944, after the liberation of the city, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. In September the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> recognised it as the legitimate provisional government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Reorganised under de Gaulle, it took in representatives of the Socialists, the Communists and other parties and began work on the constitution of the French Fourth Republic. De Gaulle resigned in 1946 over the provisional legislature's refusal to give more powers to the President and over quarrels between the factions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “French Committee of National Liberation” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…forces landed at Algiers and Oran in Operation Torch, and in 1943 Algiers became the seat of the <b>French Committee of National Liberation</b>, headed jointly by Charles de Gaulle and Henri Giraud until de Gaulle took sole control; Algerian units then fought in Italy and in the liberation…</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>That year the movement moved to Algiers and merged with Henri Giraud's forces in the <b>French Committee of National Liberation</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-france">Free France</a></li><li><blockquote>In June 1944 the <b>French Committee of National Liberation</b> in Algiers renamed itself the Provisional Government of the French Republic, led by Charles de Gaulle.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-the-french-republic">Provisional Government of the French Republic</a></li></ul></section>
